::  name       tablecloth name bound
::  shape      bytes -- int (@u32), bound 48 -- a length, with an alphabet standing beside it
::  invariant  a stored name fits forty-eight bytes and is one whole manifest field
::  example    48
::  readers    Tablecloth - Pond - Glow Tend - shape museum
::  nib        tablecloth-v1 at 20260812.142132
::
::  Tablecloth's fourth pedestal, and the last of its bounds to take one. It names max_name from
::  brushstroke/tablecloth.rye, which bounds how long an artifact's human name may be.
::
::  Why this desk shows a number AND a list. On 20260830 max_name was found doing a second job
::  nobody had written down. The Brix-facing manifest writes one line per artifact,
::  `artifact <name> <digest> <len>`, and reads it back by splitting on newline and then on space,
::  so a name carrying either byte renders a line this module's own parser refuses. The length
::  bound was never the thing preventing that: the shortest lawful artifact line is 77 bytes, so
::  at 48 no name is long enough to carry a forged one, and the grammar was safe by ARITHMETIC
::  rather than by a rule. Raise the bound for longer human names and the forgery comes back in
::  reach, with nothing in the number's own comment to warn whoever raises it.
::
::  So the wall is written down now, and what it holds is an ALPHABET rather than a length:
::
::  the bytes the manifest grammar reserves, as name_is_one_field refuses them:
::    space - newline
::  that is the whole alphabet the wall removes.
::
::  Three facts, and each can fail while the other two agree. The number equals max_name. The
::  bytes listed equal the bytes name_is_one_field actually refuses -- add a third to the wall and
::  this desk goes quiet about it until it is written here. And store_artifact still consults that
::  wall, so the invariant above stays wired rather than merely written: delete the one call and
::  the first two readings agree over a store that accepts anything.
::
::  Unlike this room's error-paths desk, the number here is not a count of the list. There, the
::  example IS the set's size, so the desk can contradict itself. Here the example is a length and
::  the list is an alphabet -- two facts, neither derivable from the other, so each is read against
::  the Rye rather than against its neighbor.
::
::  Why the wall has ONE caller, since max_name bounds two rooms. store_artifact takes a name from
::  outside and must ask; parse_manifest reads one back out of a manifest line, and gets the same
::  guarantee from the grammar instead -- the text is split on newline and then tokenized on space,
::  so a parsed name can carry neither byte before any check runs. The wall is called where a name
::  arrives unchecked and nowhere else, and the third reading below reads that one call on purpose
::  rather than counting call sites.
::
::  What this desk does not reach. Whether forty-eight is the right length, which is Tablecloth's
::  own design question. And pond/apps/spool_cloth.rye, whose store took the same wall in the same
::  shape on the same day; its bounds are Pond's to display.
+$  tablecloth-name-bound-shape
  $:  bytes=@u32
  ==
